        card("Culture note: food and meals", "Food is the basis of social life in the Philippines. There are three meals a day, with two snacks (miryenda, from the Spanish “merienda”) in between. Although there are native dishes, Philippine cuisine has not escaped foreign influences, especially Spanish. The dining table at fiestas is a United Nations of local and foreign food (American, French, Spanish and Chinese, among others) that sit alongside one another: native food and food from different countries adapted and accepted in the homes."),
        card("Notes", "Adobo in adobong manok (chicken adobo) is the Spanish word for marinade; the dish, often considered the national dish of the Philippines, has Spanish origins. Pananghalian (a contraction of pagkain sa tanghalian) and tanghalian (literally lunch) can be used interchangeably. In kesong puti, kapeng barako, pritong itlog, adobong manok, kaning itim and tirang litson the ng is a linker used when the word ends in a vowel; the na in mainit na tsaa, inihaw na isda and sabaw na munggo is a linker used when the word before it ends in a consonant. The enclitic naman introduces a topic related to what was just said: Ano naman ang hapunan mo? means “And how about your dinner?”. Ano ang sa iyo? means “What’s yours?”. Baboy also means “pig” in another context."),
